  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The equation\n\n$$\np{x}^{2}+qx-6={x}^{2}+7x-6\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. What is the value of $pq?$\n\n$pq=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ on the left-hand side is $p,$ and on the right-hand side it is $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\np=1.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $q,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nq=7.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}pq & =1\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
